orm{(I_A\otimes P_B)\ket{S_A(ho')} }^2 \geq \sum_{i,j=1}^N \abs{ raket{b_i}{g_j}}^2 \min\{g_j,  rac{1}{\delta}2^{c'}b_i \} 
onumber \ & \geq \sum_{j=1}^N g_j \left(\sum_{i|~b_i\geq \delta 2^{-c'}g_j} \abs{ raket{b_i}{g_j}}^2 ight) \geq \sum_{j=1}^N g_j(1- \delta) = 1- \delta . \quad \mbox{(using Claim~ef{claim:1})} \end{align} Using the above,  egin{align*} & \prob{	ext{Bob succeeds}|~	ext{Alice succeeds}} = \ensuremath{ \mathrm{Tr} } (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)} \ & \geq \ensuremath{ \mathrm{Tr} } (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho')} -  rac{1}{2} \onenorm{S_A(ho) - S_A(ho')} \ & = \ensuremath{ \mathrm{Tr} } (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho')} - \sqrt{1- \abs{ raket{S_A(ho)}{S_A(ho')}}^2} \quad \mbox{(Fact~ef{fact:tracefidelityequi})} \ & \geq 1 - \delta - 2 \sqrt{ (1-\mathrm{F}(ho,ho'))^{1/2}} . \quad \mbox{(Claim~ef{claim:2} and Eq.~\eqref{eq:probonrho'})} \end{align*} \end{proof} Finally, we show that if Alice and Bob succeed together on an index, the state in register $B$ with Bob is close to $ho$.  egin{claim} Given that both Alice and Bob succeed, fidelity between $ho$ and the state of the register $B$ is at least $\sqrt{1 - \delta - 2 \delta^{1/4}} \geq 1 - {\varepsilon} $ . \end{claim}  egin{proof} From gentle measurement lemma (Fact~ef{fact:gentlemeasurement}), $$\mathrm{F}(S_A(ho),  rac{(I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)} (I_A\otimes P_B) }{\ensuremath{ \mathrm{Tr} } (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)}}) \geq \sqrt{\ensuremath{ \mathrm{Tr} } (I_A\otimes P_B)\ketbra{S_A(ho)}}. $$ Since the marginal of $\ket{S_A(ho)}$ on register $B$ is $ho$ and partial trace does not decrease fidelity (Fact~ef{fact:monotonequantumoperation}), using item 2. above, the desired result follows. \end{proof} Let $j$ be the first index where Alice and Bob both succeed. As described in the protocol, $m$ is the first index where Alice succeeds and $n$ is the index such that Bob outputs the state in $B_1^n$. We have the following claim,  egin{claim} With probability at least $1-4{\varepsilon}$, $m=n=j$. \end{claim} Before proving Claim~ef{claim:highprobindex}, let us define the following 